    "body": "Stack from [ghstack](https://github.com/ezyang/ghstack/tree/0.18.0) (oldest at bottom):\n* __->__ #5399\n\nFSDP2 sets the unsharded parameter's grad_dtype to the reduce dtype. With\nthe deferred upcast, it stays fp32 only on a microbatch with gradient sync\ndisabled and no gradient yet, i.e. the first microbatch of gradient\naccumulation. There `_LinearFunction.backward` computed the WGRAD as a bf16\n`mm`, and autograd then cast it to fp32: an extra cast kernel and a\nfull-size bf16 temporary, with the WGRAD rounded to bf16 before the upcast.\n\nWithout a running gradient, the backward now reads `weight.grad_dtype` and,\nwhen it differs from the input dtype, computes the WGRAD with\n`mm(out_dtype=grad_dtype)` so the GEMM's fp32 accumulator is written\ndirectly. The branch stays because CPU has no `mm(out_dtype=)` kernel, even\nwhen the dtype matches the inputs; grad_dtype None (FSDP deferring the\nupcast) keeps the plain `mm`.\n\nMXFP8Linear (`output_dtype=ctx.wgrad_dtype`) and HiMidLoLinear (fp32 WGRAD\nin every path) already produce the WGRAD in the gradient's dtype.\n\nNumerics: unchanged without FSDP or with gradient sync on every\nmicrobatch. Under FSDP with an fp32 reduce dtype and gradient\naccumulation, the first microbatch's WGRAD is no longer rounded to bf16,\nso it is more accurate and not bitwise identical to before.\n\nTest: test_first_wgrad_is_produced_in_grad_dtype checks the Function\nreturns fp32 and matches `mm(out_dtype=fp32)` bitwise; it fails without\nthis change.",
